WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.778 --> 00:00:03.611
This is good.

00:00:03.611 --> 00:00:06.590
Rock throwing was a real knightly skill.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:07.857
Hi there. My name's Dave Rawlings.

00:00:07.857 --> 00:00:10.040
I'm the master-of-arms at
the London Longsword Academy,

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.502
which basically means I teach people

00:00:11.502 --> 00:00:12.643
to stab each other for money.

00:00:12.643 --> 00:00:14.010
I'm Toby Capwell.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.897
I'm a curator of arms and armor

00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:18.150
at The Wallace Collection
in Central London.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.140
I'm also a practitioner

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.910
of the European historical martial arts.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.600
Today, we're going to look
at duels in movie and in TV

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.725
and we're going to see
how realistic they are.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.220
This film is portraying a
highly fictionalized version

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.050
of a real historical event.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.416
A trial by combat, or a judicial duel,

00:00:41.416 --> 00:00:43.200
is a legal procedure

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.820
to settle some kind of dispute.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.480
This is a legal standing.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.510
It's an appeal to the
direct intervention of God.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.560
This is really a medieval phenomenon,

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.760
although there's stories
of one-on-one combats

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:57.785
throughout history.

00:00:59.823 --> 00:01:01.370
[screaming]

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.070
That's one of the only things that we know

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:05.507
for a fact happened.

00:01:05.507 --> 00:01:09.070
Jean de Carrouges was stabbed
somewhere in the thigh.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:10.703
A good martial artist will know that

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:12.380
that's a good place to stab someone.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.180
You can bleed out very quickly
with a wound like that.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.093
And let them bleed for a while.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.683
The armor is clunkier

00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:25.058
and more cumbersome-looking

00:01:25.058 --> 00:01:26.110
than it should be.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.830
The whole general feel
of the fight is, overall,

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.120
it's too big, too clunky.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.790
There's a lot of swinging
where there should be

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.580
a lot of refined jamming it
into, finding the little gaps,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.232
forcing, always counseling them

00:01:36.232 --> 00:01:37.782
to defend the point as you're going back.

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:47.240
This is one of the things you see a lot

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.170
in the unterhalten sections
in German treaties,

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:51.933
this idea of wrestling your opponent,

00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:52.858
first of all, to the ground.

00:01:52.858 --> 00:01:54.980
Once they're on the ground,
you work your way up them.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.630
You fall with your knee
between their legs.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.160
A lot of times when there's
rolling around on the ground

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.648
in film fights, the
other opponent will then

00:02:01.648 --> 00:02:04.610
just jump sort of belly-flop
style straight onto him,

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:05.670
which doesn't achieve much.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.830
But here you've got a
very intentional crouch

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.883
binding the fallen enemy's limbs.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.650
I guess when a lot of people
think of a knightly dagger,

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:21.250
they think of a knife that
looks a bit like a sword.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:25.830
Cross-hilted cruciform daggers
like this were a thing.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.370
Can be used blade up,

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.003
it can be used blade down,

00:02:29.003 --> 00:02:30.853
stabbing action, whatever.

00:02:30.853 --> 00:02:32.680
But actually they're not.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.862
The much more typical knightly
dagger for armored combat

00:02:36.862 --> 00:02:39.480
are daggers like the
one we see in the clip,

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.671
the so-called rondel dagger.

00:02:41.671 --> 00:02:43.113
Which, instead of a cross guard,

00:02:43.113 --> 00:02:45.430
it's got two circular plates.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.180
It's not really a weapon
that's effectively used

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.430
to extend the reach of the arm.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.604
It's really all about this.

00:02:54.604 --> 00:02:58.180
And in the clip here, you
actually see Matt Damon

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.310
getting in and leaning on it.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.850
But you do still see it
quite a lot used like so,

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.470
and, again, it's all about the utility

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:05.540
that you get out of it.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.450
You can put your weight
on this, absolutely valid.

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.300
For me, it's more about the spacing.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.590
So this gives you much
more protection than this,

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.156
particularly if you're in a position

00:03:13.156 --> 00:03:14.939
exactly as Toby said like so,

00:03:14.939 --> 00:03:17.010
you're preventing somebody from
stabbing up into your hands.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.230
In the German treaties that we study,

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.870
we call this the wrath
position or the wrath strike.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:24.210
When we think about
knights and formal dueling,

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.140
there's a question of manners, politeness.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.230
Fights to the death aren't clean.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:32.170
And the dirtiness of
it, perfectly plausible.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.150
6 out of 10.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:37.340
It's got some good references in it,

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.330
but with this production
crew at this level of budget,

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:42.730
it could have been a lot better.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.722
Yeah, I'm going to say a 5, 6 as well.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.960
It's not anything

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.720
like the typical Hollywood lance exchange.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.920
Normally, they'll charge each
other one, two, three times

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.170
and someone falls off.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:00.291
And it's really kind of formulaic

00:04:00.291 --> 00:04:02.190
and boring and predictable.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:05.190
Here, they go on the first charge,

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.100
and they both blast each other out.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.870
You can see real impact.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.910
You see the guy's lance get
banged out the back of him,

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:15.780
nearly hits one of the extras
on the side there almost.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.980
And the falls are pretty brutal, too.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.010
These giant maces,

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:25.943
which as far as I am aware

00:04:25.943 --> 00:04:28.540
are not a weapon that ever existed.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:29.950
There are two-handed maces,

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:32.610
but with these giant, oversized heads.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.710
They've basically taken a short mace

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.750
and then just enlarged
it to mythic proportions.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:41.900
'Cause there is a long
tradition in judicial combats

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.950
of using unusual, weird weapons

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:47.325
just for the trial by combat

00:04:47.325 --> 00:04:49.977
that are not like the normal weapons

00:04:49.977 --> 00:04:51.550
that you would use on the battlefield.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.740
I'd like to see people
in kind of a lower guard

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.740
without just swinging the
thing around their head.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:57.460
Swinging the thing around
your head is not a good use

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:59.110
of energy at all. It's
a waste of your time.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:00.470
It's just sort of like the big barreling,

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:01.870
"We're going to run in and do big moves,"

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:03.470
when your moves want to be concise

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:04.611
and about controlling your space,

00:05:04.611 --> 00:05:05.961
particularly when you're using something

00:05:05.961 --> 00:05:07.590
which is primarily a thrusting weapon.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.793
The thing that makes me giggle about it,

00:05:12.793 --> 00:05:14.309
but I do like it, is the way that

00:05:14.309 --> 00:05:15.780
whenever there's a swing at somebody,

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.440
they kind of try and skip out the way.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:18.850
On one level, it looks comedic.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:19.718
On the other level,

00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:21.243
well, you don't want to get
hit by something really heavy,

00:05:21.243 --> 00:05:22.460
so you might well skip out the way.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.690
Hollywood people are
forever trying to get rid

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:33.560
of their actors' helmets
as quickly as possible.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.440
There are plausible ways
of getting rid of a helmet

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.210
if you bother to think about it.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:40.190
He's having a hard time breathing,

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:41.980
he's probably overheating,

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.200
and he just panics and loses the helmet.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.990
That's a storytelling device
that I can get my hands on.

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:52.033
And actually I've suffered
from near heat stroke

00:05:52.033 --> 00:05:53.940
fighting in armor in California.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.930
And taking your helmet off
might seem like a bad idea,

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:58.730
but it's about practicality.

00:05:58.730 --> 00:05:59.800
You might get hit in the head,

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.250
but you're definitely going to lose

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.708
if you fall on the floor.

00:06:02.708 --> 00:06:04.567
The queen is innocent!

00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:06.213
[crowd shouting]

00:06:06.213 --> 00:06:11.213
Here, Gawain rescinds his
accusation of adultery.

00:06:11.213 --> 00:06:13.656
There is a real precedent

00:06:13.656 --> 00:06:16.747
for someone who's been
defeated in a judicial duel

00:06:16.747 --> 00:06:20.060
and is about to be dispatched

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:23.758
can make an admission
that they were wrong.

00:06:23.758 --> 00:06:25.310
I'm going to have to give it another 5.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:26.500
I'll give it a 6,

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.475
because it's got a
legitimate helmet removal.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:39.920
Women did actually fight judicial duels,

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:41.460
especially in Germany.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:44.070
Women historically weren't knights,

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.010
that's a fantasy element here,

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.020
but they did fight judicial duels.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.070
And later, they fought
civilian duels as well,

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:53.700
particularly in the
17th and 18th centuries.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.868
Renly: You may ask
anything of me you desire.

00:06:55.868 --> 00:06:58.293
If it is within my power, it is yours.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:01.731
Your grace.

00:07:02.642 --> 00:07:05.640
I ask the honor of a place
in your king's guard.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.573
I like this because knights often did

00:07:08.573 --> 00:07:12.489
fight formal combats for other reasons

00:07:12.489 --> 00:07:15.787
beyond the legal procedure
of a judicial duel.

00:07:15.787 --> 00:07:18.590
Sometimes they fought what
were called deeds of arms,

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:21.073
which were these kind
of exhibition fights,

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:22.631
a lot like this.

00:07:22.631 --> 00:07:26.050
And it's just about the
prestige and the fame,

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:29.020
the celebrity aspects of knighthood,

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:31.243
which is something "Game of
Thrones" does really well.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:33.913
Today is not the day I die.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:41.931
Both sides in this judicial procedure

00:07:41.931 --> 00:07:44.439
are fighting with champions,

00:07:44.439 --> 00:07:47.265
professional fighters who volunteer

00:07:47.265 --> 00:07:48.673
to act on their behalf,

00:07:48.673 --> 00:07:50.781
and that is absolutely a real thing.

00:07:50.781 --> 00:07:52.700
It's almost very much like
having your own lawyer.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:53.955
This person will come and fight for you.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:59.842
I'm not sure quite this much
exhibition jumping about

00:07:59.842 --> 00:08:01.430
is a good idea here.

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:02.955
I'm a bit wary of anything

00:08:02.955 --> 00:08:04.913
that might give your opponent information

00:08:04.913 --> 00:08:08.200
about how you move and
what you're capable of.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:09.605
I think it's potentially a good way

00:08:09.605 --> 00:08:11.260
of releasing your own adrenaline.

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:13.220
If you stand there and you're very stoic

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:14.350
and you're kind of [grunts],

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.680
then all of that adrenaline is
coursing through your system,

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:18.580
and it makes your
reactions very, very jumpy.

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:19.775
So it's almost like this feeling of

00:08:19.775 --> 00:08:21.550
freeing up your limbs, ready to fight.

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:29.750
It seems like a spear with a
wooden haft is a bad choice

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:31.750
when you've got a 7-foot-tall guy

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:33.700
with a big sword facing you.

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:35.450
I guess you don't have to let him hit it.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.147
I do like that the majority

00:08:37.147 --> 00:08:38.838
of the deflections with the spear

00:08:38.838 --> 00:08:41.430
are ones which accentuate
the movement of the sword.

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:42.560
There's good deflections.

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:45.520
There's not these attempts to
confront the weapon mainly,

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:46.760
which is very, very good.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:48.610
There's not very much of this just

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:49.868
direct threat, threat, threat

00:08:49.868 --> 00:08:51.835
changing through from
side to side to side.

00:08:54.099 --> 00:08:56.740
There is an awful lot of
movement around the opponent.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:59.230
Cavandish talk about getting
behind your opponent.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.280
Marozzo talks about
walking to their shoulders.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:03.740
Like a modern fencing
match, just up and down.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:04.820
With very few exceptions.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:06.750
You're categorically
trying to get the best,

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:09.700
most advantageous position
that takes your opponent's arms

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:11.233
out of their natural position of strength.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:19.400
The way that we portray people
who are incredibly large

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:23.400
in general forms of fiction
is massively fiction.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.900
That person, who is immensely strong,

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.066
has to be, for some reason,

00:09:27.066 --> 00:09:29.260
uneducated and unable to move.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:30.610
And this is not true.

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:34.110
You get some absolute
premium, huge athletes --

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:35.450
Toby: Who move fast.
Dave: Who are nimble.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:38.470
Yes, terrifyingly fast,
and are intelligent

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:40.360
and use that to their own advantage.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.945
It would be nice really to see a fight

00:09:41.945 --> 00:09:45.460
that represents that not
only is this person huge,

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.080
but they have studied their art.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:50.773
Oh, no, you can't die yet,
you haven't confessed!

00:09:50.773 --> 00:09:51.612
Say it!

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:56.890
He clearly has made a mistake
with all this gloating.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.680
You've got to keep your
emotions under control.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:02.560
You've got to stop with the pride

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.830
and the celebration prematurely.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.140
A knightly kind of mental
preparation is about keeping

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:12.480
a very even, kind of
dispassionate mindset.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.660
I'm going to go for a 7 1/2.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:16.510
I really, really like
the way the spear moves.

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:18.180
I hate the way the sword moves.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.160
I'd be willing to give it a 6.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.495
How many more must die for the throne?

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:24.730
Just one.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:26.790
I think you can always
look at this as just being,

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:28.250
what are your physical attributes

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:31.020
and your training relative
to those of your opponent?

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:32.830
If your endurance is good and you're older

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:34.630
or if your endurance is
good and you're younger

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:37.050
and you spend more time as
a younger person fighting,

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:39.280
it's more likely to go to the person

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.540
with the best combination
of attributes in effect.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:44.120
But the mental aspect is important, too.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:48.500
Older fighters often
have a mental advantage

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:50.860
because they're better
at keeping their cool.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:53.815
The bigger, stronger, more experienced guy

00:10:53.815 --> 00:10:55.450
is going to have an advantage.

00:10:55.450 --> 00:10:57.430
It's kind of that point of,
where are you on the curve?

00:10:57.430 --> 00:10:58.690
How many injuries have you taken?

00:10:58.690 --> 00:10:59.982
How does that affect your performance?

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:07.090
Where's the probing of it?

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:09.250
It's always, I'll step
in, and then I'll hit.

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:10.813
And that's a sure way for somebody

00:11:10.813 --> 00:11:12.863
to have the right time to do something.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.280
There's too much rolling around now.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:19.200
There's reference in a
knife-fighting manual

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.870
to basically effectively faking a trip.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:24.380
So you fall over, and then
you roll past your opponent.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:26.040
Got caught by somebody in
a sword and buckler fight

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:27.570
because they literally,
they come towards you,

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.720
they trip over their own
leg, and you go, "Ha!"

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.970
And they roll behind you and
they cut the back of your leg,

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:33.560
literally rolling along the floor.

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:36.210
So there's certainly places for it.

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:38.330
Making it the predominance
of a fight scene,

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.760
I think, is very, very, very flawed.

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.033
Does his highness need a respite?

00:11:44.213 --> 00:11:45.980
Five minutes.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:48.703
The whole thing of taking a break

00:11:48.703 --> 00:11:52.012
in the middle of a trial by battle,

00:11:52.012 --> 00:11:56.700
it's unusual and probably
normally not allowed,

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:59.940
but there is some
historical precedent for it.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:02.340
There was a famous deed of arms

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:04.490
called the Battle of the Thirty

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:07.815
in 1351 in Brittany

00:12:07.815 --> 00:12:10.623
between two groups of Breton knights.

00:12:10.623 --> 00:12:13.570
They fought very hard
for quite a long time,

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:15.640
and then they did agree to take a break.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:17.360
They were exhausted,

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:20.470
but yet they hadn't achieved a resolution.

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:23.490
So it's not totally without precedent.

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:25.437
I think it's going to be another 5, 6,

00:12:25.437 --> 00:12:28.640
just because there's so much rolling.

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:31.210
And I appreciate that the armor is nice.

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:32.703
The younger guy especially

00:12:32.703 --> 00:12:34.328
could have used more training, I think.

00:12:34.328 --> 00:12:36.023
I'm going to say 5 on that one.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:39.630
I come from a planet where
we have no understanding

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:41.263
of reach or measure. [laughing]

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.350
A lot of people have criticized this scene

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:52.340
for the slowness of the
dinosaur guy's actions,

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:54.685
but that actually relates very well

00:12:54.685 --> 00:12:58.080
to what it feels like when
you're fighting in armor.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:02.540
You can feel as though you
are moving really dynamically

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:05.250
and all these amazing
things are happening,

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.680
and then you see the camera
footage of the fight,

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:09.710
and it's just like ...

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:13.040
There's a Castilian
knight named Luis Zapata

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:15.581
who wrote a book on knightly combat

00:13:15.581 --> 00:13:17.273
at the end of the 16th century.

00:13:17.273 --> 00:13:20.920
He says specifically, when
you're in full-plate armor,

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:25.730
you must make an extra
effort to move dynamically,

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:27.100
with intention.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.080
And he says specifically

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:32.450
so that you don't look stiff as a statue.

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:35.082
You have to make that extra effort

00:13:35.082 --> 00:13:37.507
to get out of the kind of Gorn effect.

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:45.385
We get accidentally into another
piece of knightly combat,

00:13:45.385 --> 00:13:48.410
or knightly health and
fitness, if you like.

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:50.350
This is like, what have
I got to hit him with?

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:53.250
I mean, element of surprise
and moving spontaneously

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:54.768
and getting ahead of your opponent

00:13:54.768 --> 00:13:56.380
is obviously very important.

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:58.263
There's not a lot of that going on here.

00:14:00.975 --> 00:14:01.808
This is good.

00:14:01.808 --> 00:14:04.620
Rock throwing was a real knightly skill.

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:06.311
Numerous knightly treatises

00:14:06.311 --> 00:14:08.936
talk specifically about
the correct techniques

00:14:08.936 --> 00:14:10.740
for throwing small, medium,

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:13.550
and large rocks appropriately.

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:15.600
Not perhaps that large.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.530
The Gorn, as a sort of reptilian humanoid,

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:20.670
I think he's being sold to us

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:23.630
as a creature that has a very thick hide.

00:14:23.630 --> 00:14:26.000
I just like the idea he's on
his way to a cocktail party

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.104
and he's been accosted by Kirk.

00:14:28.104 --> 00:14:29.066
Absolutely 10.

00:14:29.066 --> 00:14:30.230
Is there a 12 button?

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:31.140
Yeah, 12.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:32.631
To the death!

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:43.640
It's a form of punishment
meted out on prisoners,

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:45.630
making them fight to the death.

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:48.040
And normally I would say,
what's with the whips?

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:49.873
The whip is not a weapon,

00:14:49.873 --> 00:14:52.273
but the whip is also an implement

00:14:52.273 --> 00:14:55.660
for corporal punishment,
again, on prisoners.

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:57.900
So it's kind of consistent with, again,

00:14:57.900 --> 00:14:59.820
the circumstances in the story.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:01.480
So it's more just making
two prisoners fight

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.096
for your entertainment
rather than any of them

00:15:03.096 --> 00:15:04.560
having any particular standing.

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:06.875
And fight with implements that are used

00:15:06.875 --> 00:15:09.313
to mete out corporal punishment.

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:15.430
For that kind of mannered behavior,

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:17.690
you probably would need to go forward

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:20.670
to the 17th or 18th century.

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:24.090
In civilian duels, you
might see very cordial,

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:26.780
formal politeness

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:29.760
occurring right before they
stab each other to death.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.390
Here, it's quite a theatrical thing

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:36.450
where they just emphasizing
that Flash is a good guy

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:39.730
and the prince is just
being a bit of a d---.

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:43.622
These are modern conceptions
of shaking hands and things.

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:48.842
There is a tradition,

00:15:48.842 --> 00:15:51.561
sort of theatrical fights
occurring in weird places.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:56.730
I don't think traps were ever a thing,

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:58.680
because really they're going to

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:01.171
ruin the quality of your fight.

00:16:01.171 --> 00:16:04.490
I mean, even in gladiatorial
combat in ancient Rome,

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:07.800
they're concerned about the
quality of the fighting.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.130
And if you've got people
tripping and falling over

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:11.898
and accidentally spiking themselves,

00:16:11.898 --> 00:16:14.403
it kind of ruins the spectacle.

00:16:17.208 --> 00:16:18.740
The footing is a problem.

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:22.610
In a lot of medieval combats
that I've been involved in,

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:25.720
you're wearing smooth-soled leather shoes

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:27.280
a lot of the time.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:30.550
And depending on what
surface you're standing on,

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:32.293
the footing can be a real problem.

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:36.007
Take my hand! Come on!

00:16:36.007 --> 00:16:38.333
You've won! Let him go.

00:16:39.899 --> 00:16:41.280
That's not how this works.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.900
If the opponent is
defeated but not killed,

00:16:43.900 --> 00:16:47.010
the officers of the court take
him out and hang him anyway,

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:49.590
because you have to have that resolution.

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:53.800
The fight has proven that
if he is the accuser,

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:56.450
then he has beared false witness.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:16:59.690
And if he's the accused, then he's guilty.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:01.890
Either way, he has to die.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:04.030
Historically in judicial
duels, is the penalty

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:06.670
usually death regardless,
or can it be a lesser thing?

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:10.250
For the loser in the High
and Late Middle Ages,

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:12.190
the loser dies one way or another.

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:14.540
By that point, we're dealing
with judicial combats

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:18.130
that are fought largely for
very, very serious offenses,

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:19.690
like murder or rape.

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:21.107
In the earlier Middle Ages,

00:17:21.107 --> 00:17:23.515
when they're fighting
about land ownership,

00:17:23.515 --> 00:17:25.400
they don't need to kill.

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:28.660
One person can just say, "OK,
the land's yours, I give up."

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:29.610
I'll give it a 7.

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:31.710
It just does what it wants to do,

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:34.110
and it's outrageous and camp.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:35.890
For the accuracy of portraying birdmen,

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:36.940
I'm giving it a 10.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.320
Does anyone ever use a flail on horseback?

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:46.863
It's not a good idea.

00:17:46.863 --> 00:17:51.230
Flails are massively overemphasized.

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:52.646
And when they are used,

00:17:52.646 --> 00:17:54.762
historically, they're used on foot.

00:17:54.762 --> 00:17:58.691
So that's the one kind
of letdown in this scene.

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:04.430
All this mounted combat is great.

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:06.350
I'm glad they're using rubber weapons,

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:08.240
'cause they hit the
horses a couple of times.

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:09.930
But that probably would happen,

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:11.488
and there's a reality to it.

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:16.505
The simplicity of this fight scene

00:18:16.505 --> 00:18:18.079
is that they're trying to hit the person,

00:18:18.079 --> 00:18:19.330
in general, where the shield is not.

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:20.890
And they're just looking
for every single gap.

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:22.960
If the shield's going up,
you're cracking him underneath.

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:24.800
The whole thing where they're fighting

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:27.720
with different weapons is
something of a problem.

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:30.786
A judicial duel, properly,

00:18:30.786 --> 00:18:33.560
has to occur on equal terms.

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.493
So the weapons have got to be the same

00:18:35.493 --> 00:18:36.368
one way or another;

00:18:36.368 --> 00:18:38.850
you can't choose whatever
you want to fight with.

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:42.060
That's a kind of old Hollywood trope

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:43.810
that's appeared in several films.

00:18:43.810 --> 00:18:44.810
It has to be fairness.

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:45.970
You do see mismatch though.

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:47.510
You get somebody who's got a spear,

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:48.490
somebody who's got a sword.

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:51.098
In Talhoffer, some of
the mismatched weapons

00:18:51.098 --> 00:18:53.090
is about trying to address

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:55.790
other physical inequalities.

00:18:55.790 --> 00:19:00.330
Here, you've got a proper
duel of chivalry set in 1194,

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:02.600
really the high point of judicial dueling.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:05.413
Two knights, evenly matched,

00:19:05.413 --> 00:19:07.910
should be fighting with
the same set of weapons.

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:10.190
So this is set in 1194.

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:11.800
The equipment that they're wearing

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:15.120
is more typical of the
early to mid-13th century.

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:17.210
They're a generation or so out.

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:19.510
But that's really starting to get picky.

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:22.094
Overall, this is a really great sequence.

00:19:23.313 --> 00:19:24.872
There's manipulation of the horses

00:19:24.872 --> 00:19:26.788
against each other for advantage,

00:19:26.788 --> 00:19:28.340
there's navigation around the shields.

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:30.190
So for me, it's a good solid 8, I reckon.

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:32.720
I'd rather they charged
with the lance first

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:35.150
and then progressed with other weapons,

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:37.340
but there's real mail.

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:39.150
I give it a 9 out of 10.

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:39.983
Thanks for watching.

00:19:39.983 --> 00:19:42.148
And if you enjoyed this,
you might like these ones.

